{{- /*
tmpdir: the absolute path this machine should use for TMPDIR, or empty if the
OS default (/tmp) is fine.

Rationale per machine:
  - headless (Unraid): /tmp is a plain directory on the 16G rootfs ramdisk that
    holds the whole OS, so agents cloning repos there can wedge the box. Point
    at a btrfs subvolume on the cache SSD. Swept hourly by the tmp_cleanup user
    script (unraid-config repo).
  - linux desktop: /tmp is its own tmpfs mount (systemd tmp.mount, capped at
    50% of RAM) and is already aged out by systemd-tmpfiles, so a runaway can
    only ENOSPC /tmp rather than take the system down. We still redirect big
    agent scratch to disk to stop wasting RAM. Created and swept declaratively
    by ~/.config/user-tmpfiles.d/agent-tmp.conf.
  - container: temp lives on the container's own writable layer (SSD); no
    redirect needed.
  - darwin: $TMPDIR is already a per-user dir on disk, cleaned by the OS.

Consumers: dot_profile.tmpl, dot_config/fish/exact_conf.d/tmpdir.fish.tmpl,
dot_config/environment.d/tmp.conf.tmpl,
dot_config/user-tmpfiles.d/agent-tmp.conf.tmpl.
The Unraid gmuxd launcher sets the same path independently (it runs before any
shell): unraid-config/plugins/user.scripts/scripts/{dotfiles_setup,start_gmuxd}.
*/ -}}
{{- if .container -}}
{{- else if .headless -}}
/mnt/cache/tmp
{{- else if eq .chezmoi.os "linux" -}}
{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/tmp
{{- end -}}
